## Summary

Selected and implemented **FEATURE-099** (must, deps satisfied): *Admin REST API must write audit-log entries for all mutations (review C4).*

**Problem:** All 12 mutating admin-API endpoints performed zero `AuditLogger` calls while every web-admin twin logs — a leaked admin token could delete users, reset 2FA, or revoke tokens with no forensic trace.

**Changes:**
- `src/Controller/Api/AdminApiUserController.php` — injected `AuditLogger`; added `Request` to the actions that lacked it; added a private `audit()` helper and a `$this->audit(...)` call after each of the 10 successful mutations (create, update, delete, activate, deactivate, force-logout, password-reset, unlock, revoke-tokens, reset-2fa).
- `src/Controller/Api/AdminApiInvitationController.php` — same pattern for send + resend.
- Action names reuse the web twins where they exist (`admin.user_create`, `admin.user_edit`, `admin.user_delete`, `admin.user_password_reset`, `admin.user_reset_2fa`, `admin.user_unlock`, `admin.user_revoke_tokens`, `admin.user_invite`, `admin.user_invite_resend`) and follow the same convention for the API-only endpoints (`admin.user_activate`, `admin.user_deactivate`, `admin.user_force_logout`). All record actor = acting admin, actorType `'admin'`, ip, action, outcome `'success'`. Logging only fires on success (verified by a 404-writes-no-row test).
- `tests/Functional/Api/AdminApiAuditLoggingTest.php` (new) — 13 tests, one per mutating endpoint plus the no-row-on-failure case.

